# Select the supported connectivity architecture

## Goal

Choose the adapter strategy and keep authentication on Proton-owned surfaces
without turning browser automation into part of the product security boundary.

## Completed work

- Select official Proton Mail Bridge as the supported version-one provider path.
- Reject bundled Playwright, Chromium, browser-profile scraping, and reuse of
  Proton-owned client identifiers.
- Record Proton's QR/manual-code session-fork flow as evidence for a preferred
  future external handoff using a project-specific approved client identity.
- Specify revocable session storage for any future direct handoff.
- Compare Bridge, an approved direct-session adapter, and raw credential login.
- Record account-plan requirements, platform limits, protocol coverage, and
  failure behavior.
- Keep environment-supplied credentials explicit and less recommended.
- Keep direct API work behind a separate experimental boundary.
- Define the provider-neutral port consumed by runtime composition.
- Define adapter selection, capability negotiation, and failover rules without
  ever combining partial results from different adapters silently.

## Acceptance evidence

- `docs/architecture/connectivity.mdc` accepts Bridge as the supported release
  path and blocks direct-session access on approved third-party registration.
- Authentication remains separate from MCP; raw browser state, account
  passwords, Bridge passwords, and session blobs never cross MCP traffic.
- The decision maps planned capabilities to Bridge evidence or explicit
  capability omission until equivalent semantics are proven.
- Proton's current documentation is recorded for Bridge paid-plan requirements,
  local IMAP/SMTP behavior, generated Bridge credentials, and session
  revocation.
- Proton QR sign-in documentation and pinned WebClients session-fork code are
  recorded as evidence for code-based handoff without credential replay.
- The decision explicitly forbids impersonating Proton's official client IDs.
- `MailProviderPort` and `select_adapter` define the provider-neutral selection
  boundary without dynamic dispatch in the domain.
- Synthetic tests prove that partial adapters are never silently combined and
  that ordered selection requires one complete adapter.
- Targeted domain tests, Clippy, and Jig complete cleanly.
